    <h2>3. What is EDT (Eastern Daylight Time)?</h2>
    <p>
      Eastern Daylight Time (EDT) is the daylight saving time offset observed in the Eastern Time Zone during the summer months. It operates at an offset of **UTC-4** (four hours behind Coordinated Universal Time). When it is 12:00 PM UTC, it is 8:00 AM EDT.
    </p>
    <p>
      EDT is active from the second Sunday in March to the first Sunday in November. By moving clocks forward by one hour during the warmer months, communities extend evening daylight hours, reducing energy consumption and encouraging outdoor activities.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because EDT is a daylight saving offset, it is not observed in countries that remain on permanent standard time. For instance, while New York and Toronto switch to EDT, Caribbean nations like Jamaica stay on UTC-5 year-round, resulting in a temporary one-hour time difference during the summer.
    </p>
  </section>

