  <section id="why-they-exist" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Why They Exist">
    <h2>5. Why Do EST and EDT Exist?</h2>
    <p>
      The existence of EST and EDT is directly tied to the history of Daylight Saving Time. The concept was first proposed by Benjamin Franklin in 1784 as a satirical suggestion to save candles. It was later formally proposed by New Zealand entomologist George Hudson in 1895, who wanted more daylight hours to collect insects after his shift.
    </p>
    <p>
      The United States first adopted daylight saving time during World War I to conserve coal. The practice was codified into federal law under the Standard Time Act of 1918. During World War II, the nation enacted permanent daylight saving time (known as "War Time") to save fuel.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, daylight saving rules are regulated by the **Uniform Time Act of 1966**. The law established a standardized schedule for clock transitions across the nation, ensuring that transportation schedules and business communications could operate consistently.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
