          <!-- Crossover overlap highlight -->
          <rect x="250" y="5" width="250" height="20" fill="#36c7b5" rx="4" opacity="0.6" />
          <text x="475" y="40" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#36c7b5" text-anchor="middle">4-Hour Collaborative Overlap (New York Morning / London Afternoon)</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 6: Custom crossover diagram highlighting daily collaborative hours between London and New York.</p>
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Scheduling a Webinar</strong>: A global company schedules a webinar for 10:00 AM Eastern Time in July. Because the East Coast is observing EDT (UTC-4) in July, the webinar will occur at 2:00 PM GMT. If the company scheduled the same webinar for January, it would occur under EST (UTC-5), which translates to 3:00 PM GMT.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Financial Market Openings</strong>: The New York Stock Exchange opens at 9:30 AM Eastern Time. During winter standard time (EST), this translates to 2:30 PM GMT. During summer daylight saving (EDT), this translates to 1:30 PM GMT, shifting peak market liquidity windows for European traders.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Database Systems and Server Logs</strong>: System administrators store server logs in UTC to avoid chronological issues. If a server recorded transactional entries in EST year-round, local database queries conducted during summer EDT hours would log a permanent 1-hour database skew.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 9: COMMON MISTAKES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Timezone Mistakes">
    <h2>9. Common Timezone Mistakes &amp; Pitfalls</h2>
    <p>
      Timezone abbreviations are often misused in communications, leading to calendar conflicts and scheduling errors:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Using EST in Summer</strong>: Writing "the meeting is at 2:00 PM EST" in July is a common mistake. Since July is in daylight saving time, the correct abbreviation is EDT. Using the wrong abbreviation implies a UTC-5 offset instead of UTC-4, causing confusion for international partners.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing Eastern Time with EST</strong>: Assuming that "EST" is the name of the timezone year-round is incorrect. The correct term for the zone is Eastern Time (ET). EST refers specifically to the winter offset.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calendar Double-Runs</strong>: During the autumn fall back shift in November, the hour between 1:00 AM and 2:00 AM occurs twice. If computer systems are not configured to use UTC, automated cron jobs scheduled during this period may execute twice.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
